  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 148 AM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nToday through Friday, surface high pressure departs toward the \neastern CONUS and leeward pressure falls across the High Plains will \nmaintain a steady southerly low-level return flow across the forecast\narea. Under mostly clear skies today, warm air advection and deep \ndiurnal mixing will push afternoon highs into the upper 60s to around\n70 degrees, accompanied by south winds of 10 to 15 mph. Increasing \nboundary-layer moisture and passing mid to high cloud cover tonight \nwill hold overnight lows in the low 50s across most of the area, \nthough northern valleys may dip into the mid 40s. On Friday, a \ntightening surface pressure gradient ahead of an approaching upper \ntrough will strengthen sustained south winds to 20 to 30 mph, \nstrongest across north-central Kansas near Concordia. Despite \nincreasing afternoon clouds, warm advection will again support highs \nin the upper 60s to low 70s area-wide.\n\nSaturday through Sunday, an amplifying upper-level trough and \nassociated surface cold front will sweep across Kansas over the \nweekend. Warm, moist southerly flow ahead of the boundary will keep \nSaturday morning temperatures mild in the upper 50s to near 60 \ndegrees before frontal passage initiates a sharp northwest to\nsoutheast temperature gradient. Highs on Saturday will be reached \nearlier in the day across north-central Kansas (around 59 degrees at \nConcordia) while east-central areas near Topeka and Emporia warm into\nthe upper 60s ahead of the front. Deepening synoptic lift and \nmoisture transport along and behind the front support high \nprecipitation probabilities (60% west to 95% east) Saturday. Cold \nair advection drop Sunday afternoon highs into the upper 40s to 50 \ndegrees, followed by Sunday night lows falling into the 20s.\n\nMonday through Thursday, a colder, more amplified upper pattern \nsettles across the central Plains behind the weekend system, ushering\nin below-normal temperatures through the middle of next week. \nSurface ridging and persistent northeast to north winds will \nreinforce cool, dry low-level air across the area. Overnight lows \nwill consistently drop into the 20s.",
